}}{{Politics of Bolivia}}

[[General election]]s were held in [[Bolivia]] on 18 October 2020 for [[President of Bolivia|President]], [[Vice President of Bolivia|Vice President]], and all seats in both the [[Chamber of Senators (Bolivia)|Senate]] and [[Chamber of Deputies (Bolivia)|Chamber of Deputies]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=2020-07-24|title=Bolivia election delayed to October as pandemic bites, opposition cries foul|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bolivia-politics-election-idUSKCN24O2PY|access-date=2020-07-24}}</ref> [[Luis Arce]] of the [[Movimiento al Socialismo]] (MAS-IPSP) party was elected president in a landslide,<ref>{{Cite news|date=2020-10-21|title=Official Bolivia Vote Count Confirms Socialist Landslide|language=en|work=Bloomberg.com|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-10-21/official-bolivia-vote-count-confirms-socialist-landslide|access-date=2020-10-23}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-10-21|title=Bolivia's Landslide Lays to Rest the Fears of Fraud|url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/latin-america-caribbean/andes/bolivia/bolivias-landslide-lays-rest-fears-fraud-0|access-date=2020-10-23|website=Crisis Group|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=Don't mess up Bolivia's miracle|newspaper=The Economist|url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2020/10/24/dont-mess-up-bolivias-miracle|access-date=2020-10-23|issn=0013-0613}}</ref> winning 55% of the vote and securing majorities in both chambers of the [[Plurinational Legislative Assembly]]. The results of the election superseded the disputed results of the [[2019 Bolivian general election|October 2019 elections]], which were annulled during a prolonged [[2019 Bolivian political crisis|political crisis]]. 

Although the winning party received a higher proportion of the vote in 2020 than in the previous annulled elections, for the first time since 2009 the winning party did not have a two-thirds majority in the Legislative Assembly, meaning that some functions would require cross-party support.<ref name=twothirds>{{cite web|url=https://correodelsur.com/politica/20201025_el-mas-no-alcanzo-los-2-3-en-dos-de-las-ultimas-cuatro-elecciones.html|title=The MAS did not reach 2/3 in two of the last four elections|language=es|date=25 October 2020}}</ref> For the first time, the Senate will contain a majority of female senators.<ref name="femalemajority"/> Electoral authorities had initially scheduled the elections for 3 May 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elvocero.com/el-mundo/para-mayo-las-nuevas-elecciones-en-bolivia/article_88a7220a-2e8e-11ea-b53f-6f0155fdf6ce.html|title=Para mayo las nuevas elecciones en Bolivia|access-date=4 January 2020|website=El Vocero de Puerto Rico|date=3 January 2020 |language=es-ES}}</ref> They were postponed due to the ongoing [[COVID-19 pandemic in Bolivia|COVID-19 pandemic]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.outlookindia.com/newsscroll/electoral-court-postpones-bolivia-general-election-over-virus/1775807|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200322012900/https://www.outlookindia.com/newsscroll/electoral-court-postpones-bolivia-general-election-over-virus/1775807|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 March 2020|title=Electoral court postpones Bolivia general election over virus|website=Outlook (India)|access-date=2020-03-22}}</ref> first to 6 September 2020 and then to 18 October 2020. The latter date was ratified by a 13 August 2020 law following protests and blockades against the previous postponements.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-08-14|title=Una ley fija el 18 de octubre como fecha tope para las elecciones en Bolivia|url=https://www.abc.es/internacional/abci-ley-fija-18-octubre-como-fecha-tope-para-elecciones-bolivia-202008141215_noticia.html|access-date=2020-10-17|website=ABC|language=es}}</ref>

The parties or alliances contesting the election were the [[Movimiento al Socialismo]] (MAS-IPSP), which governed before 2019, [[Civic Community]] (CC), the newly formed Creemos (''Let's create'', ''We believe'') alliance, the {{ill|Front for Victory (Bolivia)|es|Frente Para la Victoria (Bolivia)|lt=Front For Victory}} and the {{ill|Bolivian National Action Party|es|Partido de Acción Nacional Boliviano}} (PAN-BOL). Presidential candidates [[Carlos Mesa]] (CC) and [[Chi Hyun Chung]] (FPV) were the second and third-place finishers, respectively, in the annulled 2019 presidential election. [[Luis Arce]] was the MAS candidate, replacing former MAS president [[Evo Morales]]. Feliciano Mamani replaced Ruth Nina as candidate for PAN-BOL. Incumbent president [[Jeanine Áñez]], former president [[Jorge Quiroga]], and María de la Cruz Bayá all launched presidential candidacies, but withdrew before the election was held.

The official count took several days to complete. Independent quick-counts of the vote conducted by polling firms Ciesmori and ''Mi Voto Cuenta'' (My Vote Counts) on the morning of 19 October both indicated that Arce had won a majority of the vote, enough to win the election outright without requiring a runoff round.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-bolivia-election/bolivias-socialists-claim-victory-as-unofficial-count-shows-big-win-idUKKBN27407Q|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201019150739/https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-bolivia-election/bolivias-socialists-claim-victory-as-unofficial-count-shows-big-win-idUKKBN27407Q|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 October 2020|title=Bolivia's socialists claim victory as unofficial count shows big win|date=19 October 2020|publisher=[[Reuters]]}}</ref><ref name="Qcount">{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/10/19/arce-logra-mas-de-50-segun-el-conteo-rapido-de-dos-firmas-272050.html|title=Arce wins with more than 50% according to quick count of two firms|date=19 October 2020|language=es}}</ref> President Áñez confirmed this on Twitter shortly after,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/JeanineAnez/status/1318048552191483904|title=Aún no tenemos cómputo oficial, pero por los datos con los que contamos, el Sr. Arce y el Sr. Choquehuanca han  ganado la elección. Felicito a los ganadores y les pido gobernar pensando en Bolivia y en la democracia. |language=es|author=Jeanine Anez |via=Twitter|date=19 October 2020}}</ref> and former presidents Mesa and Quiroga both indicated their acceptance of the preliminary results later that day.<ref name="Quiroga">{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/10/19/tuto-arce-les-deseo-exito-ante-el-enorme-desafio-economico-que-se-avecina-272042.html|title=Tuto to Arce: 'I wish you success, given the enormous economic challenge that lies ahead'|date=19 October 2020|language=es}}</ref><ref name="MesaConc">{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/10/19/carlos-mesa-somos-la-cabeza-de-la-oposicion-actuaremos-como-tal-272066.html|title=Carlos Mesa: We are the head of the opposition and we will act as such|date=18 October 2020|language=es|access-date=19 October 2020|archive-date=26 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201026032118/https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/10/19/carlos-mesa-somos-la-cabeza-de-la-oposicion-actuaremos-como-tal-272066.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Official observers from the UN, UNIORE, and the OAS all stated that there was no evidence of fraud in the 2020 election.<ref name=nofraude/>

==Background==
===2019 election===
{{main|2016 Bolivian constitutional referendum|2019 Bolivian general election|2019 Bolivian protests|2019 Bolivian political crisis}}
{{Crisis in Bolivia sidebox}}
On 10 November 2019, after [[2019 Bolivian protests|19 days of civil protests]] following the disputed [[2019 Bolivian general election|election results of October 2019]] and the release of a report from the [[Organization of American States|OAS]], which alleged irregularities in the electoral process, the [[Armed Forces of Bolivia|military]] and the [[law enforcement in Bolivia|police]] of [[Bolivia]] forced president Morales to resign. After General [[Williams Kaliman]] made the military's request for Morales's resignation public, Morales complied, accompanied by other resignations by high-level politicians throughout the day, some citing fears for the safety of their families. The government of [[Mexico]] offered [[political asylum]] to Morales the following day, which Morales accepted a day afterwards.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-mexico-bolivia-asylum-idUSKBN1XL007|title=Mexico says it would offer asylum to Bolivia's Morales if he sought it|newspaper=Reuters|date=11 November 2019|via=www.reuters.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Bolivia crisis: Evo Morales accepts political asylum in Mexico |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50383608 |publisher=BBC |access-date=12 November 2019|work=BBC News |date=12 November 2019 }}</ref>

As Vice-President [[Álvaro García Linera]], President of the Senate [[Adriana Salvatierra]] and President of the Chamber of Deputies [[Víctor Borda]], had already resigned, there were no constitutionally-designated successors. The First Vice President of the Senate, [[Rubén Medinaceli]], had also resigned.<ref>{{cite news |last=Faiola |first=Anthony |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/bolivia-to-hold-new-elections-after-protests-and-international-criticism/2019/11/10/4778e842-03b2-11ea-ac12-3325d49eacaa_story.html |title=Evo Morales resigns as Bolivia's president after OAS election audit, protests |newspaper=The Washington Post |access-date=12 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191112003631/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/bolivia-to-hold-new-elections-after-protests-and-international-criticism/2019/11/10/4778e842-03b2-11ea-ac12-3325d49eacaa_story.html |archive-date=12 November 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Jeanine Áñez]], the Second Vice President of the Senate, was the highest-ranking official still in office and she announced that she would be willing to ascend to the presidency on an interim basis in order to call for new elections. On 12 November 2019 Áñez took temporary charge of the [[Senate of Bolivia]], thereby formally placing herself in the line of succession as acting President of the Senate, and on this basis proceeded to declare herself the Constitutional President of the country. Her accession to office was formally legitimized by a decision of the [[Plurinational Constitutional Tribunal]] later the same day, which stated that she had lawfully assumed office ''ipso facto'', in accordance with the precedent laid out by Constitutional Declaration 0003/01 of 31 July 2001.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://tcpbolivia.bo/tcp/?q=content/comunicado-1|title=COMUNICADO &#124; Tribunal Constitucional Plurinacional|website=tcpbolivia.bo|access-date=15 December 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191113165737/https://tcpbolivia.bo/tcp/?q=content%2Fcomunicado-1|archive-date=13 November 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> Many in Bolivia protested against Añez becoming president, which led to violent clashes between them and the police. Several MAS supporters were killed by the police during confrontations in Senkata and Sacaba. The Argentine delegation in Bolivia and the [[Inter-American Commission on Human Rights]] described these as massacres.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Argentine Delegation: INFORME FINAL BOLIVIA DIC 19.pdf|url=https://drive.google.com/file/d/1QEIpvaDANQvvrPQLmbXPjtck498tXohq/view?usp=embed_facebook&usp=embed_facebook|access-date=2020-10-21|website=Google Docs}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=CIDH califica de masacre lo ocurrido en Senkata y Sacaba tras que se aprobó decreto que deslindaba de responsabilidad penal a FFAA (in spanish)|url=https://www.ccb.com.bo/politica/noticia/1850/cidh-califica-de-masacre-lo-ocurrido-en-senkata-y-sacaba-tras-que-se-aprobo-decreto-que-deslindaba-de-responsabilidad-penal-a-ffaa-|access-date=2020-10-21|website=www.ccb.com.bo|language=es}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|author=Deutsche Welle|title=CIDH pide investigación internacional sobre "masacres" en Bolivia {{!}} DW {{!}} 11.12.2019|url=https://www.dw.com/es/cidh-pide-investigaci%C3%B3n-internacional-sobre-masacres-en-bolivia/a-51631043|access-date=2020-10-21|website=DW.COM|language=es-ES}}</ref>

On 20 November, Evo Morales offered to not run as a presidential candidate if he was allowed to return to Bolivia and conclude his term.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2019/11/20/evo-morales-ofrecio-no-presentarse-a-elecciones-si-le-permiten-regresar-a-bolivia-y-terminar-su-mandato/|title=Evo Morales ofreció no presentarse a elecciones si le permiten regresar a Bolivia y terminar su mandato|date=20 November 2019|work=[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]]|access-date=29 November 2019|publisher=[[Infobae]]|language=es|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191121135701/https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2019/11/20/evo-morales-ofrecio-no-presentarse-a-elecciones-si-le-permiten-regresar-a-bolivia-y-terminar-su-mandato/|archive-date=21 November 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the same day, the interim government presented a bill that aimed to forge a path to new elections. The two chambers of congress were expected to debate the bill which would annul the 20 October election and appoint a new electoral board within 15 days of its passage, paving the way for a new vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bolivia-politics/bolivia-government-proposes-election-bill-as-its-seeks-path-to-peace-idUSKBN1XU1YJ|title=Bolivia government proposes election bill as its seeks path to peace|date=20 November 2019|website=[[Reuters]]|access-date=29 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191124180427/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bolivia-politics/bolivia-government-proposes-election-bill-as-its-seeks-path-to-peace-idUSKBN1XU1YJ|archive-date=24 November 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> The bill, drafted jointly by Morales' [[Movimiento al Socialismo]] (MAS-IPSP) and anti-Morales legislators, was approved on 23 November; it prohibited Morales from participating in the fresh election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/bolivia-marks-end-of-era-as-legislators-rush-to-approve-new-election-without-morales-11574537980|title=Bolivia Marks End of Era, as Legislators Rush to Approve New Election Without Evo Morales|date=23 November 2019|website=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|access-date=29 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191129121324/https://www.wsj.com/articles/bolivia-marks-end-of-era-as-legislators-rush-to-approve-new-election-without-morales-11574537980|archive-date=29 November 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> In exchange, Áñez's government agreed to withdraw the armed forces from all protest areas (although some servicemen were still permitted to stay at some state companies to "prevent vandalism"), to revoke her decree which granted the army immunity from criminal prosecution, to release arrested pro-Morales protesters, to protect lawmakers and social leaders from attacks and to provide compensation for the families of those killed during the crisis. She approved the bill shortly thereafter.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://in.reuters.com/article/bolivia-politics-idINKBN1XY0H7|title=Bolivian leader agrees to withdraw military in deal to 'pacify' country|date=24 November 2019|work=Reuters|access-date=25 November 2019|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191128184924/https://in.reuters.com/article/bolivia-politics-idINKBN1XY0H7|archive-date=28 November 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Candidacy of Áñez ===

On 5 December, Áñez stated that she would not be a candidate or support any candidate.<ref>[https://rpp.pe/mundo/latinoamerica/bolivia-jeanine-anez-no-postulara-a-la-presidencia-segun-el-gobierno-interino-noticia-1233337 Jeanine Áñez no postulará a la Presidencia de Bolivia, según el Gobierno interino.] Publicado el 5 de diciembre de 2019. Consultado el 8 de diciembre de 2019.</ref> This was reiterated on 15 January 2020 by Minister for the Presidency [[:w:es:Yerko Núñez|Yerko Núñez]], who said that "[Áñez] will not be a candidate. The President has stated on several occasions, she will not be a candidate, this is a government of peace, transition, and management because you can not stop the state apparatus."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.la-razon.com/nacional/jeanine-anez-presidencia-gobierno-elecciones-bolivia_0_3294870527.html|language=es|title=Government clarifies that Jeanine Áñez will not be a candidate for the Presidency|access-date=16 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200122201708/http://la-razon.com/nacional/jeanine-anez-presidencia-gobierno-elecciones-bolivia_0_3294870527.html|archive-date=22 January 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Despite her previous statements, Áñez announced her candidacy on 24 January 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.atb.com.bo/pol%C3%ADtica/jeanine-%C3%A1%C3%B1ez-confirma-candidatura-presidencial-para-las-elecciones-generales|title=Jeanine Áñez confirma candidatura presidencial para las elecciones generales|date=24 January 2020|access-date=24 January 2020|website=ATBDigital|language=es-ES|archive-date=22 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022105839/https://www.atb.com.bo/pol%C3%ADtica/jeanine-%C3%A1%C3%B1ez-confirma-candidatura-presidencial-para-las-elecciones-generales|url-status=dead}}</ref> A survey reported in the Bolivian newspaper ''[[Los Tiempos]]'' indicated that, while 43% of respondents considered her to have done a "good or very good" job as interim president (compared to 27% bad or very bad), only 24% of respondents believed that she should stand as a candidate in the upcoming elections. In the same poll, 63% of respondents agreed with the statement that "as interim president, Jeanine Áñez should call elections and not take advantage of her power to become a presidential candidate."<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.lostiempos.com/actualidad/pais/20200126/encuesta-aprueban-gestion-anez-pero-rechazan-candidatura| title=Survey approves of Áñez's term as president, but rejects her candidacy.|language=es|date=26 January 2020}}</ref> On 17 September 2020, following a poll that put Arce in first place with above 40% support, and Áñez in fourth place, Áñez announced the end of her candidacy, citing the risk that the vote would be split between the conservative candidates.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/09/17/world/americas/bolivia-jeanine-anez.html|title=Bolivia's Interim President Pulls Out of Election|date=17 September 2020|agency=Reuters|access-date=17 September 2020|language=en}}</ref>

=== Election postponement and blockades ===

In March 2020, the Supreme Electoral Tribunal announced the election and preparatory activities would be postponed due to emergency restrictions to combat the COVID-19 pandemic. All major parties agreed with this measure and that a date for the new election would be chosen by the legislative assembly based on a public health evaluation at a later date.<ref>{{cite web|date=24 March 2020|title=Copa Says Legislative Will Define New Election Date After Quarantine|url=http://www.la-razon.com/nacional/elecciones-presidencia-tse-asamblea_0_3336266412.html|website=La Razon|language=es|access-date=26 March 2020|archive-date=26 March 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200326150254/http://www.la-razon.com/nacional/elecciones-presidencia-tse-asamblea_0_3336266412.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=25 March 2020|title=Parties in the electoral race avoid fixing the date of the elections; await quarantine report|url=http://www.la-razon.com/nacional/elecciones-bolivia-partidos-tse-coronavirus_0_3336866313.html|website=La Razon|language=es|access-date=26 March 2020|archive-date=26 March 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200326150254/http://www.la-razon.com/nacional/elecciones-bolivia-partidos-tse-coronavirus_0_3336866313.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On 22 June 2020, Áñez approved a law passed by both the Chamber of Deputies and the Senate to set 6 September 2020 as the date for the election, with elected authorities in place by mid-to-late November 2020.<ref>{{cite web|date=22 June 2020|title=After warning of risk, Añez promulgates the law of elections between reproaches of MAS and CC|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/6/22/tras-advertir-riesgo-anez-promulga-la-ley-de-comicios-entre-reproches-al-mas-cc-259079.html|language=es|access-date=27 June 2020|archive-date=9 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809211844/https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/6/22/tras-advertir-riesgo-anez-promulga-la-ley-de-comicios-entre-reproches-al-mas-cc-259079.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=26 June 2020|title=See the electoral calendar for 2020 elections|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/6/26/mira-las-actividades-del-calendario-electoral-para-las-elecciones-2020-259557.html|access-date=27 June 2020|archive-date=11 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200911145305/https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/6/26/mira-las-actividades-del-calendario-electoral-para-las-elecciones-2020-259557.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=3 June 2020|title=Las elecciones en Bolivia se realizarán el 6 de septiembre|publisher=NODAL|url=https://www.nodal.am/2020/06/bolivia-el-tribunal-supremo-electoral-fija-las-elecciones-generales-para-el-6-de-septiembre/|access-date=4 June 2020}}</ref>

On 23 July 2020, the [[Plurinational Electoral Organ|TSE]] announced that the election would be postponed to 18 October 2020 due to medical reports that the pandemic would have its highest peaks in late August and early September.<ref>{{Cite web|title=El Tribunal Electoral de Bolivia volvió a aplazar las elecciones presidenciales para el 18 de octubre|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2020/07/23/el-tribunal-electoral-de-bolivia-aplazo-las-elecciones-presidenciales-para-el-18-de-octubre/|access-date=2020-07-24|website=infobae|date=23 July 2020 |language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Bolivian COVID statistics|url=https://www.boliviasegura.gob.bo/datos.php|access-date=8 October 2020|website=www.boliviasegura.gob.bo|language=es|archive-date=18 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200918141741/https://www.boliviasegura.gob.bo/datos.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Evo Morales]] condemned the delays, calling them an unconstitutional attempt by Áñez to remain in power, and some groups, including the [[Bolivian Workers' Center]], carried out protests and blockades.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/evoespueblo/status/1287726662650658816|title=Condemnation|first=Evo|last=Morales|publisher=Twitter|date=2020-07-27|access-date=2020-07-28}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nodal.am/2020/07/bolivia-organizaciones-de-todo-el-pais-se-suman-a-la-marcha-de-manana-contra-la-postergacion-de-las-elecciones/|title=Bolivia: organizaciones de todo el país se suman a la marcha de mañana contra la postergación de las elecciones|publisher=Nodal|date=2020-07-27|access-date=2020-07-28}}</ref> The electoral body, experts and MAS politicians disagreed on the unconstitutionality of the decision.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/7/25/exvocales-dicen-que-tse-tiene-potestad-para-fijar-nueva-fecha-262418.html|title=Ex-officials say that the TSE has the power to fix new date.|language=es|date=25 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/7/24/el-mas-tarija-dice-estar-de-acuerdo-con-la-postergacion-de-las-elecciones-262329.html|title=MAS Tarija in agreement with postponement of elections|date=24 July 2020|language=es}}</ref> MAS supporters responded to the election postponement by protesting and, on 3 August, started an indefinite strike, blocking highways in El Alto and other party strongholds.<ref name="as120820">{{cite news |last1=Sonneland |first1=Holly K. |title=Update: Bolivia's Tumultuous Special Election |url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/update-bolivias-tumultuous-special-election |access-date=13 November 2020 |work=AS/COA |date=12 August 2020 |language=en}}</ref> Former president of Bolivia, [[Jaime Paz Zamora]] of the [[Revolutionary Left Movement (Bolivia)|Revolutionary Left Movement]], criticised Morales for supporting the blockades, which resulted in the deaths of 33 coronavirus patients due to lack of oxygen.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/8/10/jaime-paz-evo-basta-ya-has-hecho-un-enorme-dano-al-pais-en-tus-14-anos-264125.html|date=10 Aug 2020|language=es|title=Jaime Paz: Enough, Evo, you have done enough damage to the country during your 14 years.}}</ref> Morales later accepted the decision of the TSE after rising opposition to the protests.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/8/10/evo-apoya-el-18-de-octubre-como-fecha-de-elecciones-admite-que-las-protestas-no-tienen-apoyo-264075.html|title=Evo supports 18 Oct as date of elections and admits protests have no support.|date=10 Aug 2020|language=es}}</ref> On 10 August, Morales called for dialogue and asked MAS supporters to "consider" the TSE's plan for an election on 18 October with the UN as observer.<ref name="as120820"/> A survey by Mercados y Muestras showed that 59% of those surveyed believed that MAS were behind the medicine and food shortages to the cities and 65% believed that the organisers of the blockades should face prosecution.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/9/16/59-responsabiliza-al-mas-por-los-bloqueos-de-agosto-268270.html|title=59% blame MAS for August blockades.|language=es|date=16 September 2020|access-date=28 September 2020|archive-date=20 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920150512/https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/9/16/59-responsabiliza-al-mas-por-los-bloqueos-de-agosto-268270.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> MAS Vice President Orlando Zurita said the road blockades were established by MAS.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/8/8/admiten-que-el-mas-esta-detras-del-bloqueo-de-carreteras-263895.html|date=8 August 2020|language=es|title= They admit that MAS is behind road blockades.}}</ref> In some places, dynamite was used to destroy the highway and two soldiers were severely beaten by protestors.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://eldeber.com.bo/pais/bloqueadores-propinan-una-brutal-golpiza-a-dos-militares-en-sayari_196200|title=Blockers carry out brutal beating on two soldiers in Sayari|date=16 August 2020|language=es}}</ref> In Santa Cruz, Potosí, Cochabamba and other areas of the country, the police and government stated that some protesters were armed and that violence had been carried out violence against police and property.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://eldeber.com.bo/santa-cruz/policia-revela-que-ataque-en-samaipata-fue-bien-organizado-y-muestra-armas-y-objetos-decomisados_195438|title=Police reveal that attack in Samaipata was well-organised and show arms and confiscated objects|language=es|date=10 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.elalteno.com.bo/bolivia/20200830/gobierno-investiga-procedencia-de-armas-exhibidas-durante-los-bloqueos|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210615044255/http://www.elalteno.com.bo/bolivia/20200830/gobierno-investiga-procedencia-de-armas-exhibidas-durante-los-bloqueos|url-status=dead|archive-date=15 June 2021|title=Government investigate source of arms displayed during blockades|language=es|date=30 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.elalteno.com.bo/seguridad/20200812/grupos-armados-se-incrustan-en-los-bloqueos-de-la-cob|title=Armed groups embed themselves in the COB blockades|language=es|date=16 August 2020|access-date=28 September 2020|archive-date=20 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920114846/http://www.elalteno.com.bo/seguridad/20200812/grupos-armados-se-incrustan-en-los-bloqueos-de-la-cob|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 10 August, Añez asked the La Paz prosecutor general's office to investigate Morales, Arce, Choquehuanca, and eight other MAS leaders for fomenting "terrorism, genocide, and other crimes against health" in relation to the protests.<ref name="as120820"/>

In late August and early September 2020, the police reported large seizures of weapons, dynamite and ammunition which the government alleged were linked to Mexican drug cartels and MAS-affiliated groups.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://eldeber.com.bo/pais/carteles-mexicanos-tendrian-relacion-con-armas-y-dinero-incautado-segun-gobierno_199927|title=Mexican drug cartels linked to seized arms and money, says government.|language=es|date=9 September 2020}}</ref> Government supporters made statements in the media suggesting that groups related to organized crime could seek to destabilize the coming elections.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://eldeber.com.bo/pais/temen-que-se-busque-desestabilizar-las-elecciones-hallan-gran-cantidad-de-armas-dinamita-y-dinero_197755|title=They fear that some seek to destabilize elections. Large amount of weapons, dynamite and money seized.|date=27 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/9/7/analistas-hay-grupos-armados-por-la-permisividad-del-mas-267075.html|title=Analysts: Armed groups due to the permissiveness of MAS.|date=7 September 2020|language=es}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
{{Main|Elections in Bolivia}}

The President of Bolivia is elected using a modified [[First-past-the-post voting|first-preference plurality]] system ([[two-round system|a two-round system]]): a candidate is declared the winner if they receive more than 50% of the vote, or over 40% of the vote and are 10 percentage points ahead of their closest rival.<ref name="BBC50055725">{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50055725|title=Will Bolivians give Evo Morales a fourth term?|date=20 October 2019|work=BBC|access-date=21 October 2019|language=en-GB|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191020151045/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50055725|archive-date=20 October 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> If neither condition is met, a run-off election is held between the two top candidates.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.eldeber.com.bo/bolivia/El-Tribunal-Electoral-define-la-eventual-segunda-vuelta-para-el-15-de-diciembre-20190329-0103.html|title=El Tribunal Electoral define la eventual segunda vuelta para el 15 de diciembre|website=El Deber|access-date=2 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190402132651/https://www.eldeber.com.bo/bolivia/El-Tribunal-Electoral-define-la-eventual-segunda-vuelta-para-el-15-de-diciembre-20190329-0103.html|archive-date=2 April 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

The 130 members in the [[Chamber of Deputies of Bolivia|Chamber of Deputies]] (''Cámara de Diputados'') are elected using a [[seat linkage]] based mixed [[Compensation (electoral systems)|compensatory]] system using two votes: 63 deputies are elected by [[First-past-the-post voting|first-preference plurality]] to represent single-member [[electoral district]]s, 60 are elected by [[closed list]] [[party-list proportional representation]] from party lists on a departmental basis (in districts of varying sizes corresponding to Bolivia's nine departments with a threshold of 3%).<ref name="RegimenElectoral2">{{cite web |title=Bolivia: Ley del Régimen Electoral, 30 de junio de 2010 |url=http://www.lexivox.org/norms/BO-L-N26.xhtml |access-date=10 February 2015 |website=Lexivox}}</ref> The list seats in each region are awarded proportionally based on the vote for the presidential candidates, subtracting the number of single-member districts won (to provide [[mixed-member proportional representation]]). The remaining seven seats are reserved indigenous seats elected by the ''[[usos y costumbres]]''. A voter can only vote in one of either the normal constituencies or special constituencies ([[Coexistence (electoral systems)|coexistence]]).<ref name=RegimenElectoral>{{cite web|title=Bolivia: Ley del Régimen Electoral, 30 de junio de 2010|url=http://www.lexivox.org/norms/BO-L-N26.xhtml|website=Lexivox|access-date=8 October 2020}}</ref> Party lists are required to alternate between men and women, and in the single-member districts, men are required to run with a female alternate, and vice versa. At least 50% of the deputies from single-member districts are required to be women.

The [[Senate of Bolivia|Chamber of Senators]] (''Cámara de Senadores'') has 36 members, four from each the country's nine departments, which are also elected using closed party-lists, using the [[D'Hondt method]].<ref name="RegimenElectoral"/> The senate seats are also awarded based on the vote for president.

The election uses the same votes to elect the President (first round), the Chamber and the Senate, making it a [[Double simultaneous vote|double (triple) simultaneous vote]]. Voters may therefore not [[Split-ticket voting|split their ticket]] between these elections, but they may vote for a candidate of a different list in the election of the Chamber as the deputies from the single-member districts are elected using separate votes.

Voting in Bolivia is compulsory for all adults over the age of 18. The voter is given a card when they have voted so that they can show proof of participation. The voter would not be able to receive their salary from the bank if they cannot show the proof of voting during three months after the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thenews.com.pk/print/343889-voting-is-compulsory-in-some-countries|title=Voting is compulsory in some countries|date=20 July 2018}}</ref> This can result in a relatively common occurrence of invalid ballots.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Politically Unengaged, Distrusting, and Disaffected Individuals Drive the Link Between Compulsory Voting and Invalid Balloting|url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/political-science-research-and-methods/article/politically-unengaged-distrusting-and-disaffected-individuals-drive-the-link-between-compulsory-voting-and-invalid-balloting/9DDF631F6ADDE16907A75C37B7FC3AFE|journal=Political Science Research and Methods|first=Shane P. |last=Singh|volume=7|issue=1|date=January 2019|pages=107–123|doi=10.1017/psrm.2017.11|s2cid=157099704|url-access=subscription}}</ref> This is reflected in the percentages of blank/null votes under "would not vote" in the electoral surveys.

On 17 October, the TSE announced that the new preliminary count system (Direpre) would not be used because the preliminary data would not offer certainty about the results, and only the final results will be published officially. The TSE also stated that all people will be able to observe the counting process and any citizen would be able to take photos of the electoral records. Political organisations with delegates would also have the right to copies of the minutes, the calculation and scrutiny would be carried out in public, and photos of the minutes would be part of the released data.<ref>{{cite news |title=Organismos internacionales toman nota de la suspensión intempestiva del Direpre |url=https://www.la-razon.com/nacional/2020/10/17/organismos-internacionales-toman-nota-de-la-suspension-intempestiva-del-direpre/ |access-date=18 October 2020 |work=La Razón {{!}} Noticias de Bolivia y el Mundo |date=17 October 2020 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=TSE determina la suspensión del Direpre |url=https://www.paginasiete.bo/nacional/2020/10/17/tse-determina-la-suspension-del-direpre-271891.html |access-date=18 October 2020 |work=Pagina Siete |date=17 October 2020 |language=es}}</ref>

==Presidential candidates==
As of 28 January 2020, ten candidates had officially stated their intention to run for the presidency.<ref>(By candidate)
; Luis Arce: {{cite web|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2020/01/19/evo-morales-anuncio-que-luis-arce-sera-el-candidato-a-presidente-del-mas-en-las-nuevas-elecciones-de-bolivia/|title=Evo Morales anunció que Luis Arce será el candidato a presidente del MAS en las nuevas elecciones de Bolivia|access-date=19 January 2020|website=Infobae|date=19 January 2020 |language=es-ES|ref={{harvid|Infobae|2020}}}}
; Carlos Mesa: {{cite web|url=https://www.france24.com/es/20191203-carlos-mesa-se-postula-como-candidato-a-los-pr%C3%B3ximos-comicios-de-bolivia|title=Carlos Mesa se postula como candidato a los próximos comicios de Bolivia|access-date=23 December 2019|date=3 December 2019|website=France 24}}
; Luis Fernando Camacho: {{cite web|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2019/12/02/el-lider-civico-luis-fernando-camacho-firmo-un-acuerdo-preliminar-con-el-mnr-con-miras-a-las-elecciones-en-bolivia/|title=El líder cívico Luis Fernando Camacho firmó un acuerdo preliminar con el MNR con miras a las elecciones en Bolivia|access-date=31 December 2019|date=2 December 2019|website=Infobae|language=es-ES}}
